@@ -0,0 +1,20 @@
+## Context
+
+The list keeps tasks in a `Map` keyed by title (`src/domain/list.ts`). Project rules: code under `src/domain/` must not use the clock or randomness (no random identifiers) and must not throw. See proposal.md for motivation.
+
+## Goals / Non-Goals
+
+- Goals: stable, unique, deterministic ids; id lookup everywhere a title is accepted.
+- Non-Goals: persistence, ids unique across lists, changing the title-keyed storage.
+
+## Decisions
+
+- **Ids come from a per-list counter** (`task-1`, `task-2`, …), never decremented, so ids are unique, never reused after removal, and deterministic. Alternative: UUIDs — rejected, randomness is forbidden in the domain.
+- **`createTask` takes the id** as a parameter (`createTask(input, id)`), keeping it pure; the list owns the counter.
+- **Resolve a reference by title first, then by id.** An id index (`Map<id, title>` or a scan) maps an id to the task. Alternative: id first — rejected, it could make an existing title unreachable if it looks like an id. Trade-off: a task titled like another task's id shadows that id for title-accepting methods; `get(id)` is unaffected and unambiguous.
+- Rename and `store` keep the task's `id` (spread copy), so no extra work is needed there; the id index is keyed by id and updated on add/remove/rename.
+
+## Risks / Trade-offs
+
+- [Title shadows an id] → documented precedence; `get(id)` always exact.
+- [Existing tests compare whole tasks] → update them to include `id`.
